Welcome to our
session on extending

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:19.850
Anthos to manage VM workloads
in on-premises environments.

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:22.430
I'm Jennifer Smith, Director
of Product Management

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:24.620
within Google Cloud Anthos.

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:27.980
I'm also joined today by my
colleague Amr, Senior Product

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:30.340
Manager for Google Cloud Anthos.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:32.822
Amr, want to say
hi to everybody?

00:00:32.822 --> 00:00:34.030
AMR ABDELRAZIK: Hi, everyone.

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:35.290
Thank you for joining us.

00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:37.582
We're excited to show you
some of the cool things we've

00:00:37.582 --> 00:00:39.673
been working on over the
last couple of months.

00:00:39.673 --> 00:00:40.840
JENNIFER SMITH: Thanks, Amr.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:42.280
We'll talk to you again soon.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:45.070
And like Amr said, we're really
excited to share with you some

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:48.040
of the recent work we've
been doing to help enterprise

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:50.260
customers further
when they navigate

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:54.080
their hybrid and
multicloud applications.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:57.520
I know virtual presentations and
conferences have been our norm

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:00.370
for longer than we all
might have imagined,

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:03.640
so we've constructed our
session today as part talk

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:06.560
and part do via a product demo.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:09.650
So let's get going.

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:11.780
Customers are
increasingly looking

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:15.860
to modernize, whether that
modernization is in place, lift

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:18.590
and shift, or via
containerization.

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:21.050
An overwhelming
majority of enterprises

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:23.840
have a hybrid and
multicloud strategy.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.180
Wanting to modernize
with consistency

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:31.560
across all environments isn't
just a nicety, it's crucial.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:34.710
And yet even with this
increasing desire to modernize,

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:37.950
only about one sixth
of enterprise workloads

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:39.540
are containerized.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:43.230
That leaves many millions
of non-containerized VM

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:46.360
based workloads still
in the data center.

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:50.557
How can we include these VMs
in modernization efforts?

00:01:50.557 --> 00:01:51.765
We'll come back to that soon.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:58.700
Let me start by providing
a brief overview of Anthos.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:01.730
Anthos is Google's hybrid
and multicloud platform,

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:04.700
providing a consistent
operating system for Cloud

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:07.700
native deployments
across all site types,

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:12.620
whether that's on-premises,
at the edge, or public Cloud.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:16.250
It gives you all the tools you
need for consistent operations,

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:19.010
service and policy
management, so that you can

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:22.160
build once, and run anywhere.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:24.080
Starting with
container management,

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:25.970
it's a reliable
and efficient way

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:29.360
to deploy and run containers,
leveraging our experience

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:32.720
with Google Kubernetes Engine,
the industry leading container

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:34.790
management platform.

00:02:34.790 --> 00:02:37.850
On top of this sits the
service management layer.

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:41.630
It's a managed service mesh
offering to connect, manage,

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:44.400
and secure VMs and containers.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.370
This is based on Istio.

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:50.870
And on top of that is the
application development layer,

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:55.250
comprising a wide selection of
production-ready development

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:58.190
solutions and services
from both Google

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:01.490
and our partners all available
through the Google Cloud

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:02.420
marketplace.

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:12.080
The layers are flanked on two
sides by policy management,

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:14.690
offering a central place
to manage configuration

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:17.060
and security policies
across your fleet,

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:20.090
using the power of
Kubernetes along with getops

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:23.600
to implement an infrastructure
as code approach to your Cloud

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:27.890
native network, and on the
other by operations management,

00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:30.110
offering an integrated
logging and monitoring

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:35.490
service for containers,
services, and applications.

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:38.070
There were a few really
important attributes

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:40.980
we felt were essential to
building this new platform

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:43.560
to help customers with
their hybrid and multicloud

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:45.330
applications.

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:49.560
We wanted it to be open
source, declarative in nature,

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:53.350
developer friendly,
and extensible.

00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:56.100
Because of this,
we chose Kubernetes

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:59.320
as the basis of our
Anthos platform.

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:01.890
And as we've been working
with many Anthos customers

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:04.710
on their modernization
journeys, we've

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:06.525
received some very
important feedback.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:12.690
Customers have shared with us
some of their key challenges

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:16.529
with modernization, starting
with the simple fact that there

00:04:16.529 --> 00:04:20.550
are many workloads that cannot
be easily or immediately moved

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:24.510
to containers or
VMs in the Cloud.

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:27.090
They also have
organizational, skills,

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:29.940
and process gaps between
Cloud native and traditional

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:31.440
operations.

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:34.860
Their infra teams lack
unified management, security

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:37.470
and monitoring for
VMs and containers.

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:39.870
And their dev teams are
constrained and using

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:43.050
modern application development
techniques and tools

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:45.960
with traditional VM workloads.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:50.130
Delving a little bit deeper, the
root causes of these challenges

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:52.470
are long, multi-step
provisioning,

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:57.060
fragmented observability,
manual compliance enforcement,

00:04:57.060 --> 00:05:00.610
and an expensive
proprietary ecosystem.

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:01.620
So what can we do?

00:05:04.330 --> 00:05:06.730
Currently, there are
three Cloud-centric

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:10.690
and one on-premises option
for modernizing existing VM

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:12.100
applications.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:14.530
You can migrate virtual
machines to Google Cloud

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:17.110
VMs with Migrate
for Compute Engine.

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:20.230
We can lift and shift your
entire virtualization stack

00:05:20.230 --> 00:05:22.840
with Google Cloud VMware Engine.

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:24.610
You can migrate
your VM workloads

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:28.660
to containers on Cloud
with Migrate for GKE.

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:31.870
Or you could decide to modernize
to containers on-premises

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:34.120
with Migrate for Anthos.

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:37.540
We support all of these
incremental VM modernization

00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:42.080
strategies, but our customers
want another option.

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:46.120
They want a way to leave
apps in VMs until and if they

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:49.510
can be migrated, while gaining
the same benefits of running

00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:52.690
those VMs on Kubernetes
and mapping those VMs

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:55.300
using a consistent
Kubernetes-style approach

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:59.320
to provisioning, configuration,
monitoring and logging,

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:02.510
security, and optimization.

00:06:02.510 --> 00:06:06.970
So today we're adding a
new on-premises option.

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:10.540
Leveraging Google's
leadership in Kubernetes,

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:13.180
you can modernize
VM applications

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:17.260
without needing to containerize
or move to Cloud right away,

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:20.590
and you can do this now
with Anthos for VMs.

00:06:20.590 --> 00:06:24.280
Anthos for VMs lets
customers apply the expertise

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:27.460
they're building and running in
managing containers to their VM

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:31.420
environments to build bridges
of automation between containers

00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:34.900
and VMs and to reduce
their spending on VM

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:37.480
platforms and management tools.

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:41.320
With this new option, we provide
the flexibility of choice

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:47.120
to modernize in a way that
best meets your business needs.

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:49.450
So how does it
actually come alive?

00:06:49.450 --> 00:06:52.570
We've created two
adoption models, Shifable

00:06:52.570 --> 00:06:54.640
and Attachable.

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:58.540
In the Shiftable case,
VMs run on Anthos.

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:01.435
In the Attachable scenario,
we manage other hypervisors

00:07:01.435 --> 00:07:04.000
VMs with Anthos.

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:06.250
You can choose between
the two approaches

00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:08.830
or use them both simultaneously.

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:11.770
You get unified management,
declarative deployment,

00:07:11.770 --> 00:07:16.120
and policy enforcement for
your VMs and your containers.

00:07:16.120 --> 00:07:18.880
You bring a Cloud-like
experience to your VMs

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:23.350
and can leverage Cloud native
services for your VM workloads.

00:07:23.350 --> 00:07:26.290
Moving to a bit of a deeper
look at the technologies

00:07:26.290 --> 00:07:29.950
that enable the Shiftable and
the Attachable approaches.

00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:33.820
First, we see the runtime
layer with Anthos VM runtime

00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:37.600
based on Kubevirt in
the Shiftable scenario.

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:41.630
In the Attachable track,
we use VMware vSphere.

00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:45.380
Next we have network security
and policy via Anthos Service

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:47.150
Mesh based on Istio.

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:51.980
And moving up the stack, we have
Kubernetes APIs and an Anthos

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:54.660
config and policy management.

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:57.720
At the top of the stack, we
have Cloud native capabilities

00:07:57.720 --> 00:08:01.620
and services, such as patch
management, databases,

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:03.660
and AIML.

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:06.720
So with Anthos for
VMs, we help ease

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:08.790
many of the
modernization challenges

00:08:08.790 --> 00:08:11.920
our customers have
shared with us.

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:15.640
Using Kubernetes, we're
able to provide fast, secure

00:08:15.640 --> 00:08:19.960
infrastructure provisioning,
unified operational visibility,

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:24.620
automated compliance,
and cost reduction.

00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:26.930
I'm gonna pass it over to
Amr now, ask him to come back

00:08:26.930 --> 00:08:29.660
and join us and help explain
how we can get started.

00:08:29.660 --> 00:08:32.360
And you can get started
with answers for VMs

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:34.429
and run us through what
the demo will look like.

00:08:34.429 --> 00:08:35.159
Amr?

00:08:35.159 --> 00:08:36.659
AMR ABDELRAZIK:
Thank you, Jennifer.

00:08:36.659 --> 00:08:40.520
So the first step is
to do a fit assessment.

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:44.000
This is a tool that actually
understands your infrastructure

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:47.870
and tries to understand
what is the right migration

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:49.610
or what is the
right modernization

00:08:49.610 --> 00:08:52.000
journey for your application.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:54.282
It runs as a
standalone tool or you

00:08:54.282 --> 00:08:56.240
can run it on top of an
existing Anthos cluster

00:08:56.240 --> 00:08:57.770
if you're an Anthos customer.

00:08:57.770 --> 00:09:00.680
And it does an assessment
of the VM workloads.

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:03.620
And it decides on a
score that tells you

00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:06.680
what is the right journey,
whether it's Shiftable,

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:09.800
you want to be able to migrate
it to the Anthos VM runtime,

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:13.400
if it's Attachable, connecting
this VM to Anthos Management

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:15.560
Layer, or if it's
Containerizeable,

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:17.630
whether this VM is
containerized or not.

00:09:17.630 --> 00:09:21.110
And if a VM has multiple
options, it lays that out too.

00:09:21.110 --> 00:09:24.230
And there is a fit score that
also gives you the effort,

00:09:24.230 --> 00:09:25.730
and if there is
any data that you

00:09:25.730 --> 00:09:30.230
need to address, for example,
in order to move from one path

00:09:30.230 --> 00:09:31.130
to the other.

00:09:31.130 --> 00:09:34.580
From a workload perspective, you
download the standalone tool,

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:36.740
you connect to existing vCenter.

00:09:36.740 --> 00:09:39.350
It generates a local report
that, optionally, you

00:09:39.350 --> 00:09:42.080
can upload to the Cloud for
a detailed visualization

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:43.770
or historical view.

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:45.270
We'll go to the
next slide, please.

00:09:45.270 --> 00:09:48.020
This is how the fit
assessment report looks like.

00:09:48.020 --> 00:09:51.140
It provides you a data center
wide readiness, as I mentioned,

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:55.460
Actionable VM level suggestions,
ROI analysis, and risk metrics

00:09:55.460 --> 00:09:57.110
to every migration.

00:09:57.110 --> 00:09:59.540
And now next we have a demo--

00:09:59.540 --> 00:10:01.430
and we're very excited
to show you that demo.

00:10:01.430 --> 00:10:03.590
We've been working on
some of these technologies

00:10:03.590 --> 00:10:04.950
for a couple of months.

00:10:04.950 --> 00:10:07.640
So just to describe the demo,
the first part of the demo

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:10.310
talks about the discovery and
assessment using the mfit tool

00:10:10.310 --> 00:10:11.790
that we just mentioned.

00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:14.480
And then we're going to showcase
two scenarios of Shiftable

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:19.250
VM and Attachable VM that have
been migrated or attached.

00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:22.400
And then for day two we're
going to talk about management

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:25.700
from Cloud Operations, Anthos
Configuration Management,

00:10:25.700 --> 00:10:27.380
and Anthos Service Mesh.

00:10:27.380 --> 00:10:30.793
So with that, let's
move on to the demo.

00:10:30.793 --> 00:10:32.960
SPEAKER: All right, we're
now ready for a quick demo

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:35.030
of Anthos for VMs.

00:10:35.030 --> 00:10:37.350
Here we see the
Anthos landing page.

00:10:37.350 --> 00:10:38.870
This is the central
interface where

00:10:38.870 --> 00:10:41.450
you can view your
registered Anthos clusters,

00:10:41.450 --> 00:10:44.270
alter your services with
Anthos Service Mesh,

00:10:44.270 --> 00:10:47.100
or manage legacy workloads
with Anthos for VMs' new VM

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:48.577
Management Capabilities.

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:55.620
The critically important first
step when using Anthos for VMs

00:10:55.620 --> 00:10:58.290
is to run a fit assessment on
your legacy VMware environment,

00:10:58.290 --> 00:11:01.410
determine which VMs can be
easily containerised, shifted,

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:02.970
or attached.

00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:05.160
Anthos for VMs' fit
assessment tooling

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:06.960
quickly scans your
VMware environment

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:09.480
and collects application,
hardware, networking,

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:12.360
and storage metadata
related to each VMware VM

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:16.560
to systematically determine
which category it belongs to.

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:18.630
Once its assessment
is completed,

00:11:18.630 --> 00:11:20.970
the results are displayed
in a report that explains

00:11:20.970 --> 00:11:24.280
each recommendation in detail.

00:11:24.280 --> 00:11:26.582
OK, now with that
explanation out of the way,

00:11:26.582 --> 00:11:28.540
let's run a fit assessment
and see the results.

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:34.830
Here we go in Terminal, it has
Anthos for VMs fit assessment

00:11:34.830 --> 00:11:37.440
tooling installed on it.

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:40.320
After we run a single command
and authenticate our VMware

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:42.630
environment, we'll see
the assessment tool start

00:11:42.630 --> 00:11:44.880
to rapidly scan
through over 2,000 VMs,

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:47.490
and then upload it's results
to the Google Cloud console.

00:11:54.100 --> 00:11:56.060
Once a report has
been generated,

00:11:56.060 --> 00:11:59.780
we see that we've
assessed 2,077 VMs.

00:11:59.780 --> 00:12:02.270
15 of them were categorized
as being excellent candidates

00:12:02.270 --> 00:12:04.513
to attach to Anthos
for VMs with an agent,

00:12:04.513 --> 00:12:05.930
and the rest were
great candidates

00:12:05.930 --> 00:12:08.720
to be hosted on
Kubernetes using Kubevirt.

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:11.360
In particular, we
see detailed results

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:14.900
for three legacy VMs, a
front end, a back end,

00:12:14.900 --> 00:12:17.750
and a transaction service.

00:12:17.750 --> 00:12:20.970
We'll examine a few of
these results in more depth.

00:12:20.970 --> 00:12:23.550
And later on in the demo,
we'll use Anthos for VMs

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:25.380
to both shift and
attach these legacy

00:12:25.380 --> 00:12:28.440
VMs to Anthos' modern
Kubernetes-based control point.

00:12:32.220 --> 00:12:35.760
Now opening the result for
legacy front end service,

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:38.240
we'll see that it has been
categorized a shift candidate,

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:40.365
due to its Kubevirt compatible
application profile.

00:12:49.340 --> 00:12:52.490
Opening the result for the
legacy transaction service,

00:12:52.490 --> 00:12:55.138
we see it as being categorized
an attach candidate

00:12:55.138 --> 00:12:57.680
due to the fact that it requires
multiple network interfaces.

00:13:09.710 --> 00:13:13.460
OK, now it's time to generate
the migration artifacts

00:13:13.460 --> 00:13:15.975
that Anthos for VMs will use
to take control of these legacy

00:13:15.975 --> 00:13:16.475
VMs.

00:13:18.963 --> 00:13:20.380
Here we see a
migration job called

00:13:20.380 --> 00:13:21.880
demo1 is already underway.

00:13:24.760 --> 00:13:27.097
Clicking on Create
Migration will show us

00:13:27.097 --> 00:13:28.930
the prompt that was
used to create this job.

00:13:33.380 --> 00:13:37.070
By filling in just a few values,
such as migration source,

00:13:37.070 --> 00:13:39.830
operating system type
for the source VMs,

00:13:39.830 --> 00:13:43.610
and the desired output from
migration, like containerize

00:13:43.610 --> 00:13:46.700
or convert to a Kubevirt
VM, one can easily

00:13:46.700 --> 00:13:47.990
create a VM migration job.

00:13:57.280 --> 00:13:59.652
Examining our in-progress
migration job more closely,

00:13:59.652 --> 00:14:02.110
we can see that it's currently
preparing some VM management

00:14:02.110 --> 00:14:02.800
artifacts.

00:14:06.688 --> 00:14:08.480
Switching back to the
Google Cloud console,

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:11.060
we see an empty table.

00:14:11.060 --> 00:14:12.620
After clicking the
Refresh button,

00:14:12.620 --> 00:14:15.680
we see that our Anthos cluster
now contains the new Kubevirt

00:14:15.680 --> 00:14:18.140
and Istio custom resources
that are just created

00:14:18.140 --> 00:14:19.220
by Anthos Config Manager.

00:14:26.570 --> 00:14:28.755
We navigate to
the Workloads tab,

00:14:28.755 --> 00:14:31.130
we can see at the Kubernetes
pods associated with the two

00:14:31.130 --> 00:14:36.760
legacy VMs that we shift to
Kubevirt are now ready as well.

00:14:36.760 --> 00:14:38.890
Click on of these
workloads takes us

00:14:38.890 --> 00:14:41.680
to an overview page that shows
real time metrics and events.

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:48.010
Clicking on the
Operations button

00:14:48.010 --> 00:14:52.120
opens a side panel, displays
tons of additional information,

00:14:52.120 --> 00:14:54.910
like regular metrics and
logs specifically designed

00:14:54.910 --> 00:14:57.148
to assess application
reliability.

00:15:12.100 --> 00:15:16.700
Next we shift our focus to the
Anthos for VMs Service Mesh.

00:15:16.700 --> 00:15:18.440
Here we see the entire
service topology

00:15:18.440 --> 00:15:21.690
of our example application.

00:15:21.690 --> 00:15:23.800
Our example app
generates client traffic

00:15:23.800 --> 00:15:25.800
that is sent to a modern
containerized front end

00:15:25.800 --> 00:15:26.300
service.

00:15:28.890 --> 00:15:31.110
This front end service
in turn sends a request

00:15:31.110 --> 00:15:33.870
to a few other modern
APIs and our three shifted

00:15:33.870 --> 00:15:35.055
and attached legacy VMs.

00:15:38.680 --> 00:15:40.750
The front end service
is highly available.

00:15:40.750 --> 00:15:43.360
It sits behind a load
balancer, which spreads traffic

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:44.665
across five service replicas.

00:15:49.430 --> 00:15:52.460
Request to the modern front end
made to a few particular end

00:15:52.460 --> 00:15:57.060
points are automatically routed
to the legacy shifted from it.

00:15:57.060 --> 00:16:00.120
Similarly, the
modern front end also

00:16:00.120 --> 00:16:03.083
routes a few specific API
calls to the legacy shifted

00:16:03.083 --> 00:16:03.750
backend service.

00:16:07.190 --> 00:16:09.560
The legacy attached
transaction service

00:16:09.560 --> 00:16:12.410
is called both by the shifted
back end service and a number

00:16:12.410 --> 00:16:13.340
of other modern APIs.

00:16:16.080 --> 00:16:18.780
Double clicking on the
shifted back end service

00:16:18.780 --> 00:16:21.940
takes us to a detailed view
of its local service topology,

00:16:21.940 --> 00:16:23.940
where we can see info on
its current performance

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:28.470
and resource utilization,
and lots of other stuff.

00:16:28.470 --> 00:16:32.190
More importantly, now that our
legacy VM is managed by Anthos,

00:16:32.190 --> 00:16:34.350
we can now use Anthos
observability tooling

00:16:34.350 --> 00:16:37.230
to make the old app awesome.

00:16:37.230 --> 00:16:40.470
To demonstrate this we're going
to go ahead and create an SLO,

00:16:40.470 --> 00:16:42.706
AKA a Service Level Objective.

00:16:46.440 --> 00:16:51.310
First, we select an
availability-based SLI.

00:16:51.310 --> 00:16:54.920
Here we'll set our compliance
period to one week,

00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:59.350
and a performance goal
to 99.5% availability.

00:16:59.350 --> 00:17:00.640
Finally, we click Create.

00:17:06.390 --> 00:17:08.160
Next we'll add an
alert to our SLO.

00:17:11.069 --> 00:17:14.818
Here we'll just select all
the default settings, and then

00:17:14.818 --> 00:17:15.485
a short message.

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:24.920
Awesome.

00:17:24.920 --> 00:17:26.780
We've just sprinkled
some Google SRA magic

00:17:26.780 --> 00:17:29.260
on our legacy application.

00:17:29.260 --> 00:17:31.590
And now we've reached
the end of our demo.

00:17:31.590 --> 00:17:34.680
We've seen how Anthos or VMs can
be used to consistently manage

00:17:34.680 --> 00:17:37.920
legacy applications using
Kubernetes awesomeness,

00:17:37.920 --> 00:17:39.600
and slick Google tooling.

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:41.928
Enjoy.

00:17:41.928 --> 00:17:43.720
AMR ABDELRAZIK: We hope
you liked the demo.

00:17:43.720 --> 00:17:46.120
And with that, I'll
hand it over to Jennifer

00:17:46.120 --> 00:17:49.105
to tell us how we can learn
more about Anthos for VMs.

00:17:49.105 --> 00:17:50.230
JENNIFER SMITH: Thanks Amr.

00:17:50.230 --> 00:17:52.150
Thanks for sharing how
our customers can get

00:17:52.150 --> 00:17:54.310
started with Anthos for VMs.

00:17:54.310 --> 00:17:58.000
Like Amr said, we would love
to work with you as well.

00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:00.130
Anthos for VMs is
currently in preview,

00:18:00.130 --> 00:18:02.650
and we are working with a group
of customers as our design

00:18:02.650 --> 00:18:03.580
partners.

00:18:03.580 --> 00:18:06.860
If you're interested in our
preview, or learning more,

00:18:06.860 --> 00:18:09.580
please contact us at this email.

00:18:09.580 --> 00:18:12.290
We would really love
to talk some more.

00:18:12.290 --> 00:18:13.490
Thank you very much.

00:18:13.490 --> 00:18:14.500
Have a great day.

00:18:14.500 --> 00:18:17.700
And enjoy the rest of Next '21.

